WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.708
Here are two files that repeat the

00:02.708 --> 00:05.117
same calculation. I open both to see

00:05.117 --> 00:07.525
where that repeated work lives. The practical

00:07.525 --> 00:09.934
question is simple: when this behavior needs

00:09.934 --> 00:12.342
a fix, will someone remember to update

00:12.342 --> 00:13.718
every copy of it?

00:14.518 --> 00:16.756
I change one of the copies and

00:16.756 --> 00:18.994
save it. The other file has not

00:18.994 --> 00:21.231
changed with it. That is the maintenance

00:21.231 --> 00:23.469
problem repeated code can create: a fix

00:23.469 --> 00:25.707
in one place does not automatically reach

00:25.707 --> 00:27.624
the other places that need it.

00:28.424 --> 00:30.639
I compare the files again to see

00:30.639 --> 00:32.853
the difference I just introduced. This view

00:32.853 --> 00:35.067
gives me something concrete to review with

00:35.067 --> 00:37.281
the team. I can point to the

00:37.281 --> 00:39.495
changed line, check its purpose, and decide

00:39.495 --> 00:41.709
whether the other copy needs the same

00:41.709 --> 00:42.025
change.

00:42.825 --> 00:45.208
A finding does not mean I should

00:45.208 --> 00:47.591
delete a copy automatically. I inspect both

00:47.591 --> 00:49.975
versions and the places that use them.

00:49.975 --> 00:52.358
If a shared implementation makes the code

00:52.358 --> 00:54.741
easier to maintain, I can make that

00:54.741 --> 00:57.124
change and test the behavior I intend

00:57.124 --> 00:57.805
to keep.
